# The CGT Atlas — book project master copy

**What this is.** A book project: *The Combinatorics & Graph Theory Atlas* — a self-contained combinatorics/graph-theory book written lecture-course style ("Feynman edition": intuition and story before formalism, complete proofs, graded exercises with machine-verified worked answers, verified computational labs, and an HTML "web edition" as the standard delivery format for every chapter). Built over 78 working sessions by deep Mity (org deepsudden). The master plan, session log, to-do lists, and editorial conventions all live in the atlas file listed below.

**Why these files are here.** Canvases do **not** persist into a new chat — each new chat starts with an empty canvas area. This Knowledge topic is the durable master copy of every project file. A new chat must restore the canvases from here before doing any project work.

## Resume protocol (for a new chat)

1. **Read this file first.**
2. **Restore the canvases.** For each `cgt-*.md` file in this folder â the 17 chapter masters + `cgt-atlas.md` + `cgt-publishing.md` + `cgt-ch1-web.md` (20 canvases) â create the folder `/home/user/canvases/<name>/` and copy the file in as `CANVAS.md` (byte-identical, e.g. by `cp`; verify with `diff`). Example: `cgt-ch2-graphs.md` â `/home/user/canvases/cgt-ch2-graphs/CANVAS.md`. There are 37 files (20 cgt-*.md + 16 plain .html exports + KNOWLEDGE.md), ~4.2 MB total â the light set (session 78): the 15 per-chapter web-edition frames were retired (every frame's payload was byte-identical to its chapter master and the renderMd v2 engine, md5 d5eaa031â¦, is identical across frames, so only `cgt-ch1-web.md` survives as the canonical frame/engine master). The .html exports (cgt-ch1-web.html through cgt-ch16-web.html) are fully pre-rendered plain-HTML pages â the chapter content baked in as real HTML, no script, no encoded payload, readable in any browser even with JavaScript disabled â and since session 78 they ship CLEAN: no export banner, no Revision History appendix, no chapter-status footer (that development record lives in the chapter canvas only). No canvas restore needed for them. Do not edit the copies in this folder during the session â treat the restored canvases as the working set. (The Ch. 1 figures companion was retired at user direction, session 33: master copy removed here, live canvas tombstoned â the platform does not support canvas deletion; its figures live byte-identically in-text in Ch. 1.)
3. **Open the atlas canvas** — "The Combinatorics & Graph Theory Atlas — Master Plan & Progress Report" (`cgt-atlas.md`) — and read its §3 session log (all rows, latest first in the table's lower half) and §5 conventions.
4. **Follow the working protocol written there:** open the atlas first; add the next session-log row marked IN PROGRESS *before* any authoring; do **one unit per session**; update the atlas after every unit; verify every claim by machine before acting; a chapter edit ⇒ rebuild and re-verify its web edition; at close, replace the IN PROGRESS row with the full record. **For long multi-step sessions (adopted session 27, user direction): track step-by-step progress in a temp Knowledge topic buffer (e.g. `cgt-<session>-buffer`) — files in /home/user do NOT survive restarts; update the buffer after every completed step and sync each edited canvas over its master here; delete the buffer topic at close.**
5. **Before the chat ends, refresh this folder** (see the stale-copy rule below).

